course: dictionary
lesson: 11991
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is its context. «At the wedding, the guests popped the champagne and made a toast.» Here the clues are popped and toast, so champagne is a drink.
explain: The same word can name a colour. «Mia painted her bedroom walls a soft champagne.» Walls are not drunk, so champagne must mean a pale golden-cream colour.
explain: Champagne can also be the name of a place. «Champagne is in north-east France.» Try each meaning in the sentence. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: Champagne is a sparkling white or pink wine, drunk at celebrations such as weddings and New Year.
fact: Champagne is also a pale golden-cream colour.
fact: Strictly, only wine made in the Champagne region of France may be called champagne. People sometimes use the word loosely for any fizzy wine.
fact: Champagne is an adult drink, and children do not drink it.
fact: «A champagne lifestyle» means very rich living.
exercise: «At the wedding, the guests raised their glasses and drank champagne.» What is champagne here? | A kind of bread | A pale golden-cream colour | A sort of bicycle | *A sparkling wine for celebrations
exercise: «Mia painted her bedroom walls a soft champagne.» What does champagne mean here? | *A pale golden-cream colour | A loud noise | A bright red pen | A fizzy drink for grown-ups
exercise: Which sentence uses champagne as a place? | *«Champagne is a region in north-east France.» | «Pour me a glass of champagne.» | «The walls were champagne.» | «We will pop the champagne at midnight.»
test: «They lived a champagne lifestyle, with fancy cars and a yacht.» What does champagne lifestyle mean? | A fizzy drink for children | *Very rich living | A kind of quiet tent | A slow walk in the park
test: A good way to check the meaning of a word like champagne is to: | Guess the longest meaning you can think of | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Read only the first word of the sentence | Skip the sentence and never come back to it
exam: «At New Year, the adults made a toast with champagne, and the children had juice.» What does champagne mean here? | A place in France | *A sparkling wine for adults | A pale golden-cream colour | A type of cake
